abstract: >-
  Reproducible experimental framework implementing a controlled kernel-swap
  protocol for comparing classical and quantum kernels under distribution
  shift, instantiated on EMBER malware detection and on UNSW-NB15 and ToN-IoT
  network-flow scenarios (label-conditional tail stress tests and a held-out
  attack-campaign shift), with SVC and Laplace Gaussian-process classifiers.
  Under target-label-free ID-validation selection, per-configuration
  regularization tuned on training data alone, structurally matched candidate
  budgets, and inference by fixed case studies with pointwise conditional
  intervals, no consistent evidence of a fidelity-kernel
  out-of-distribution advantage is observed. A within-generation factorial,
  entangling-versus-product circuit stratification, budget and effective-rank
  matching sensitivities, prospectively defined external corroboration on
  three TableShift domain shifts, and repeated finite-shot perturbations
  accompany the comparison.

keywords:
  - quantum machine learning
  - kernel methods
  - distribution shift
  - out-of-distribution robustness
  - kernel-target alignment
  - malware detection
  - network intrusion detection
